A||||22||BAM-BAM||I have a Caster question?||||8||
Z||000000||BAM-BAM||08-05-2000||05:36 PM||||  I finaly get the suspension finished and take it down for an alignment. I go back two hours later only to find that they couldn't do it because the the cross caster was to far out, 2.56*. The alignment guy told me that the passanger side was back and the driver side was forward and that I would have to gain an inch overall to get it even close. Does this sound right? That seems like alot of deviation. Is there a fix? where do I start. I don't like the idea of my frame being bent. What about those little washers on the inside of the upper control arm? You know the ones, they are about 3/16 to 1/4 inch thick. When I took the pivot bolt out to change the bushings they fell on the floor and I, in my infinte wisdom, didn't pay attention to there correct placement. They looked the same, so I figured it was arbitrary. There were no other shims or washers on the inside and just the two big washers on either end of the pivot bolt. I need some help people, this has been the most frustrating week of my life. Well, there was that time when I was sixteen...nevermind. TIA for any and all advice or help.<P> Late, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT||||reg||8
Z||000001||BAM-BAM||08-05-2000||08:23 PM||||C'mon, there has to be a suspension expert out there somewhere with extensive knowledge on alignment proceedures. I need help and in a hurry. <P> Thanx, BAM!<P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT||||reg||9
Z||000002||terryk||08-05-2000||08:29 PM||||I ran into the same issue. <P>I took it to the dealer. They were familiar with actually removing the bolt and reversing the washers.<P>The alignment shops want to crank the toe and move the struts and get money. When faced with a *real* alignment, they choke. I had to actually explain to them how it works. I left quickly.<P>The two washers are different thickness. By swapping position you can go from +1 to -1.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-05-2000||08:37 PM||||Unless the car has been hit.........<P>From what you are describing I'd look at the craddle bushings first. Those can throw the whole rear of the car way off. Put in polly ones. (The fact he said the passenger side rear is off is a pretty good clue. That side takes more engine beating than the left.) I'd replace them even if the look ok to the eye. They aren't all that expencive, just a pain to put in.<P>He definatly did the right thing telling you to get it fixed vs trying to compensate for it. Which you really can't do anyway in a Fiero. If he was able to adjust for it you car would end up going down the road slightly sideways. Not a good thing.<P>As for the front end work.... I don't think that those washers are the problem. Remember that the whole front is off simply because you had it apart. With an inch+ problem I'd start with craddle bushings and see if that brings it around to where the front is in range. If the washers on the pivot bar are diferant thicknesses I can't see it, but then I can't go look to check either.... So it's posible.... Are we all talking about the same washers?<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||BAM-BAM||08-05-2000||09:38 PM||||    Ogre, The cradle bushing theory sounds reasonable. They told me that they had to set the rear to get accurate measurements on the front. So, that being the case, and if the cradle bushings affect rear alignment, there in could lie the anomaly. I'm sure that the cradle bushings are shot. Just about every other bushing and mount on this car is in bad shape, save the new polys on the suspension. The way that it read was; <BR>---------------------------------------------<BR>                    FRONT<P>CAMBER LEFT= .47*<BR>CAMBER RIGHT= -.23*<BR>CROSS CAMBER= .70*<BR>CASTER LEFT= 6.22*<BR>CASTER RIGHT= 3.66*<BR>CROSS CASTER= 2.56*<BR>TOE LEFT= .62*<BR>TOE RIGHT= -2.60*<BR>TOTAL TOE= -1.99*<BR>---------------------------------------------<BR>                    REAR<P>CAMBER LEFT= -1.15*<BR>CAMBER RIGHT= -.04*<BR>CROSS CAMBER= -1.11*<BR>TOE LEFT= .16*<BR>TOE RIGHT= .30*<BR>TOTAL TOE= .46*<BR>THRUST ANGLE= .07*<BR>---------------------------------------------<P> I am assuming by the numbers that these are the prealignment settings. I'm not sure, I have never been involved in this sort of thing before. Please, you people with the knowledge, review and evaluate. I truly appriciate any and all help. I was saving  the cradle bushings for my engine swap, but then, I was saving the new rear callipers for my brake mod. Oh well!<P> Thanx, BAM<P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||08-05-2000||09:43 PM||||After I had reworked my entire suspension I went to one place that said they couldn't do it because my frame was bent in front causing my caster to be wacked. I drove across the street to a brand new Goodyear shop. They inspected the car and told me it needed a total alignment and they could do it but I had to pay the hourly rate since it wasn't a minor adjustment. They added washers to the upper control arm and adjusted my upper ball joints (MOOG slotted)<BR>to bring my caster exactly in. It took over 4 hours for the total 4-wheel alignment and cost me over $200.00 but the alignment came out to exact specs. I even have a 2-page report of before readings, after readings and the actually washer sizes they changed to. My opinion is find a real alignment shop that has Caster washer kits and an experienced alignment guy. It will cost more than their standard alignment price but well worth it. My alignment came out perfect and they did a great job. By the way you didn't accidently mix the upper control arms (left/right)?<P>By the way I just looked up my caster specs They had 2.35 (L) and 1.98 (R) my final readings are 5.8 (L) and 5.6 (R) spec is 3.0 min 7.0 max<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 08-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||08-05-2000||09:56 PM||||Ooops, forgot to tell you. The car was broad-sided by a Mac truck and all of the bushings are shot......<P>Well, in that case, no, the washers might not fix it.....||||reg||12
Z||000007||Oreif||08-05-2000||10:00 PM||||Bam Bam you must have posted while I was typing. Your caster specs are within the limits. Do you know if it was an old machine or a new one? Below is the specs for all.<BR>FRONT<BR>Caster min 3.0* max 7.0*<BR>Camber Min -0.3* max 1.3*<BR>Toe min .05* max 0.25*<BR>Total Toe min 0.15* max 0.50*<BR>SAI should be set to 7.5*<P>REAR<BR>Camber min -1.5* max -0.5*<BR>Toe   min -0.1* max 0.1*<BR>Total Toe min -0.2* max 0.2*<BR>Thrust angle less than -1.0*||||reg||1
Z||000008||BAM-BAM||08-06-2000||12:04 AM||||   Oreif, I am assuming that the equipment is new since the shop is a brand new Big-O. As for how current the equipment is I can't say as I know nothing about these things. If I'm not mistaken Stimpy's father is a Big-O guy isn't he? I'll have to give a shout out to my bud Stimp and see what his pops has to say on the matter. Ogre's theory of the cradle bushings sound like a likely possibility also. I should have know with all the problems that arose durring the installation of my new suspension that I wouldn't get off without at least one more hitch and some more new parts. But, on the positive side some things are getting done sooner than I had anticipated.That means that some things I had planned on doing in the next couple of months will have to take a back seat. So all in all, it's a wash.<P> Thanks Guys, BAM!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||08-06-2000||12:59 AM||||Big-O was the place that was lost. "Actually take the bolt out and exchange washers? We've never seen that! Sorry, can't do it. That take time......"<P>They do a great job on standard quick-money struts. One guy quietly admitted that they pray that the Fiero fronts are in spec so they don't have to deal with them. It's not that hard but they didn't want to keep the washers on hand.<P>||||reg||13
Z||000010||BAM-BAM||08-06-2000||01:20 AM||||  TK, Do you happen to know what the specs are for the Fiero front end? Are the specs I posted within?(not doubting you Oreif) Hell, I thought that they were being genuinely honest when they said that there was a problem. Could it be they were just being lazy? It's my understanding that caster generaly is nonadjustable. Is there an adjustment for caster on the Fiero? If you have any answers I would appriciate it. Also keep in mind that this is an 85GT with Eibachs. Does that make a huge difference?<P> Thanx, BAM!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P> P.S. Where could I get a caster washer kit for a fiero?<P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT||||reg||7
Z||000011||BAM-BAM||08-06-2000||01:20 AM||||  TK, Do you happen to know what the specs are for the Fiero front end? Are the specs I posted within?(not doubting you Oreif) Hell, I thought that they were being genuinely honest when they said that there was a problem. Could it be they were just being lazy? It's my understanding that caster generaly is nonadjustable. Is there an adjustment for caster on the Fiero? If you have any answers I would appriciate it. Also keep in mind that this is an 85GT with Eibachs. Does that make a huge difference?<P> Thanx, BAM!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P> P.S. Where could I get a caster washer kit for a fiero?<P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT||||reg||7
Z||000012||terryk||08-06-2000||01:32 AM||||I'm not an alignment expert. I'm just reflecting on experience.<P>Not handy, but I can dig them up and compare them. I know GM has updated them a couple of times and there are many versions floating around. Orief's certainly look like what I have (somewhere). The caster is pretty loose.<P>The manual (the real manual) shows the two washers. One is 3mm and other is 9mm thick. By swapping them, you move the upper control arm forwards or backwards thereby changing the caster. Changing the bushings can throw this way off since the inner sleeve may be in a slightly different position. At this point, different thickness washers are needed. Any GOOD shop will have access to them. I don't know a direct source.<P>Barring any real chassis problems, yes, *lazy* is the word I would apply. They have to support the lower control arm, remove the bolt, swap or exchange the washers, replace the bolt, reset the alignment system and repeat until it's right. Then when it's not right, they hate it. At the same time, the specs are so wide on caster that it's not hard if they have the washers.<P>When I installed the Eibachs on my Ciera GT, I watched the guy literally throw wrenches "cause this thing is WIPED OUT!!". He was pissed because he couldn't do a quick alignment in 20 minutes. Geez, it took him 45 to bring it back in. Tough. Then I made him shim the rear axle bearings to align it. I thought blood was going to spurt from his eyes.<P>God help you if you don't have slotted upper ball joints.......<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||BAM-BAM||08-06-2000||01:03 PM||||  Oreif, I pulled this from one of your previous posts <BR>Left or right front<BR>Camber: min = -0.3* Max = 1.3*<BR>Caster: min = 3.0* Max = 7.0*<BR>Toe: min = -0.1* max = 0.1*<P>Total Front<BR>Cross camber: min = -1.6* max = 1.6*<BR>Cross caster: min = -2.0* max = 2.0*<BR>Total toe: min = -0.2* max = 0.2*<P>Rear left or right<BR>Camber: min = -1.5* max = -0.5*<BR>toe: min = -0.1* Max = 0.1*<P>Total Rear<BR>Total toe: min = -0.2* max = 0.2*<P>Note * = degrees<P> I was concerned about the difference in the toe numbers on the front being different from what you posted previously on this thread. Was this a typo or a change? Just want all the facts before I start any thing.<BR> Also, I read in a archived post that the rod that the upper control arm mounts on is adjustable for both caster and camber pretty much simultaniously. Is there anymore info on this? Here is what was posted;  <P><BR>There are two bolts that attach the rod the upper A-arm pivots on to the frame. They are adjustable in and out, fore and aft. The problem is you have to adjust for caster AND camber at the same time.  Try to change just the caster and nine times out of ten the camber moves also. Its virtually impossible to get perfect, but a 1/4 degree total tolerance is acceptable and not hard to get if you know what you're doing.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<P>How do I get that "ORIGINALY POSTED" thing going?<BR>  <P> Thanx, BAM!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<p>[This message has been edited by BAM-BAM (edited 08-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||stimpy||08-06-2000||01:58 PM||||Bam-Bam, if you look at the top of each post you'll see an icon of a page with an arrow pointing away from it. That will allow you to quote.<P>That being said... In defense of Big-O dealers, be aware that each store is <B>independantly owned and operated</B>. It is the franchise owners decision on what equipment to buy, what kind of staff to hire, etc. One bad experience shouldn't queer the deal for all dealers.  I know my dad made a <B>considerable</B> investment in opening his Big O store and purchased all state of the art equipment.  But someone still has to actually do the work. If you are not satisfied with what the technician has to say, ask to speak with the <B>owner</B>. You don't have to deal with a manager telling you what their policy is.  Talk to the guy with the big fuzzies and tell him what you need.  Customer satisfaction is a big deal for people like my dad.  It's what pays the bills.<P>Anyhow, I'll try to pass this info on to my dad. I know he's been having some technological difficulties with his 'puter. I'll try to bring back some good info.  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||terryk||08-06-2000||02:22 PM||||Oops. The Fiero alignment was the only problem I've had with Big-O. The wrench throwing was another store.<P>I didn't intented to imply a sweeping condemnation of Big-0. The quality of the business is as good as the management.<P>The Big-O in Auburn just didn't want to take the bolt out and align the caster.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Oreif||08-06-2000||08:50 PM||||Bam Bam the specs I listed above in this thread are the updated specs. I think it was Ogre who told me that the others I posted were the old specs. The differences are listed in a Tech Bulletin. I think Ogre knows which TB it is since he is the one who mentioned it. You can also look it up at a pontiac dealership once you find out the number. ||||reg||1
Z||000017||PJ||08-06-2000||09:56 PM||||Bam Bam:<P>I was replying to someone with an '88 in the post you quoted. Unless you have an '88 the info I posted doesn't apply.<BR>As far as alignment specs go there are no magic numbers. Front caster is sorta a personal preference. More positive equals more straight line stability and steering return at the cost of more effort turning the wheel. Front & rear negative camber #'s can be determined by  monitoring if the tires are wearing evenly inside vs outside. Toe-in or out is another personal preference. Some like a little toe-in some like a little toe out. You always need a little toe-in at the rear unless you like the rear end coming loose when you reach 80+ MPH. Rear toe-out may be advantageous racing autocross though.<BR>Everyone's chassis is different. An important point is to get the numbers close left and right. Figure out what works for you and fine tune it.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR>  <p>[This message has been edited by PJ (edited 08-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Big O||08-07-2000||12:26 AM||||I think I need to put in at least my two cents worth in defense of the Big O name.  Yes, Stimpy is right.  I did put a lot of money into the equipment I bought for my shop.  My alignment equipment cost me more than my first house did, but it wouldn't be worth a dime to me if I didn't have the services of an excellent mechanic who isn't afraid of any car I want to put on the rack.  The only cars we turn away are those with obvious collision damage that has tweaked the frame.  My guy has 18 years experience in the business and is the best.  I'm sorry to hear that someone had a bad experience at their local Big O store.  As Stimpy said, you need to talk to the man in charge to settle the problem if it is a problem.  Alignment problems should never be tackled in somebody's back yard or garage.  It needs to be done by a competent mechanic with the proper training and equipment.  Unfortunately that's sometimes hard to do. My suggestion is that you keep looking until you find the right place, or bring it to me in Modesto.||||reg||1
Z||000019||BAM-BAM||08-07-2000||01:05 AM||||   Big O <BR> I wasn't bashing BIG-O or anyone else. As a matter of fact I thought that it was pretty cool of them to let me know that there was a problem and not charge me. They were very proffesional and courtious. My question to you was if the numbers that I posted seemed like a problem. So far my deduction is that the caster on both sides should be extremely close to equal and better a little on the positive side. I was thinking around 5.5*. Also, does the  rear alignment affect the caster on the front in any way? I can't see it, but then again I'm not an "EXPERT". Is there a caster washer kit available? Or is my next stop a frame shop. I'm in Vegas and Modesto is looking quite feasible. It might even make a nice little road trip. Well, thanks Big O, sorry if I left the wrong impression on anyone(not intended that way). Let me know if you have any wisdom to empart.<P> Thanx, BAM!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000020||BAM-BAM||08-07-2000||01:51 PM||||  Ok, I called some other alignment shops around town and was told to call an alignment supply company that they all use. I did and was told that there exist no caster kits of any kind for Fiero's. So now my question is, can I simply use ordinary washers and add or subtract as needed? I think I can handle that if it is an acceptable practice. Some one please help me out here, I'm getting desperate. Modesto is sounding like a good bet right now.<P> Late, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT||||reg||8
Z||000021||Oreif||08-07-2000||03:06 PM||||Bam Bam I don't think you are going to find a "kit" for a Fiero. I was under the assumption that the washers were just available for any GM car. GM most likely didn't use a special diameter bolt for the upper control arms. Also since the 2 washers are different as previously mentioned, Why not support the front end and pop out the bolts? You can then measure the washer thickness and arrange them accordingly.<BR>From Terryk ~<BR>" The manual (the real manual) shows the two washers. One is 3mm and other is 9mm thick. By swapping them, you move the upper control arm forwards or backwards thereby changing the caster. Changing the bushings can throw this way off since the inner sleeve may be in a slightly different position. At this point, different thickness washers are needed. Any GOOD shop will have access to them. I don't know a direct source."<P><BR>Fieroshop, Do you use washers to correct caster? Do they come in kits or do you just buy standard sizes?||||reg||1
Z||000022||webbee||08-07-2000||03:28 PM||||On finding a good alignment shop. <P>I always ask at the machine shop, who does good alignments. They usually deal with the racing community who usually know where to go. If the machinest doesn't know, he usually knows who to call. Frame shops usually also have good alignment machines/personel. Again ask around. <P>||||reg||1
